Z||000000||bad911||03-19-2001||11:24 AM||||Hello... what a cool site for a Fiero newbie to find! Need a source for some quick-get-me-up-to-speed engine info. I understand the 3.1 is the same block(?) and saw mention of a 3.4 stroker. What crank is that? Is 3.4 the max then? Are there bigger V6's that will slip right in? I would even like to consider a V8 if I thought it wouldn't bastardize the car (pardon the strong wording but I know how radical conversions can go overboard-- in the old days I had a 140+hp Corvair-powered Beetle... let me tell you about "oversteer") ;-)  I need a pretty fair jump though... drove future son-in-law's new Boxter last night and need to show the kid the old man can still rock on a shoestring... I am an engine builder, so any tips on going fast and keeping it relatively streetable will be appreciated, especially in power-curve application for the auto-magic, fuel system injectors and pressure regulator, and engine management. Thanks ["You ask a lot, my friend..."] a bunch! <P>------------------<BR>Fiero newbie, 87 SE V6 auto||||reg||6
Z||000001||Oreif||03-19-2001||11:49 AM||||Welcome to our forum! If you still want it to look stock, Get a 3.4L engine out of a newer camaro or firebird. With roller rockers, headers, and a decent cam you can get into the 200-225hp range. The engine block is the same size externally and the Fiero valve covers and intake system will bolt right on. It is basically a drop in swap. The only machine work required it to drill and tap holes on the other side of the block to move the starter. A 3800 with supercharger is another conversion you can do. It requires more work/modification but it's a screamer. (245-285hp).<BR>V-8's are not bad swaps. There are 2 kinds. The small block chevy 350 or the northstar v-8. V-8 Archie is on here and he can answer any Q's you may have on V-8's. His website is:  <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  <BR>The least expensive/least amount of modifications is the 3.4L.<P><BR> ------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||onfire||03-19-2001||01:21 PM||||Don't forget the the 4.1, 4.5, and the 4.9 Caddilac motors. They are gaining in popularity. Check out Bubbajoes website.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||03-19-2001||01:22 PM||||Welcome to the forum! I think you'll find a wealth of info here.  <P>And just to let you know before everyone starts shouting it, check out the search feature.  All the forums posts are still online (and available on CD) and most any topic has been covered at least to some degree. Especially engine swaps and performance upgrades.  Check that out, and if you still don't find what you're looking for, just ask.  ||||reg||10
Z||000004||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||01:27 PM||||welcome. 3.1 is basicly a stroked 2.8. all your assessories will bolt rite up like OEM. You may have to redrill starter holes to mount starter on other side too. If you order a new one make sure they know its for a Fiero so they know about the starter holes. Ive heard lots of members who like the 3.4 better. Im satisfied with my 3.1. Of course you never have enough. Id like to have a 450hp 12 cyl Ferrari engine myself, lol.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
